          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em direct category
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            in which the morphisms only go in “one direction.”
          %p
            A direct category can be thought of as a category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+shapes+for+higher+structures"} geometric shapes for higher structures
            which includes only the “inclusions of faces,” not any “degeneracy” maps. (The more general notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reedy+category"} Reedy category
            can also include degeneracies.) The objects of a direct category admit no nontrivial automorphisms, but the notion can also be generalized to allow for such automorphisms.
          %p
            Alternately, a direct category can be thought of as a combinatorial representation of a
            %em single
            geometric shape. In this case, each object is a “face” and each morphism is the inclusion of a lower-dimensional face in a higher-dimensional one. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is a direct category regarded as a category
            %em of
            geometric shapes, then each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\in D
            can be represented by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D/d
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D/d
            may admit more automorphisms than
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
          %p
            Finally, a direct category can also be thought of as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            of the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-founded+relation"} well-founded relation
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#standard_definition Standard definition
          %p
            A category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is a
            %strong direct category
            if the following
            %em equivalent
            conditions are satisfied.
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              contains no infinite descending chains of nonidentity morphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo →
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo →
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo →
                    %mo ⋅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots \to\cdot \to\cdot\to\cdot
              (including cycles of length
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gt 0
            %li
              The relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≺
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\prec b
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ob(D)
              defined by “there exists a nonidentity morphism from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              to
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-founded+relation"} well-founded
            %li
              There exists a function
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi ob
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi Ord
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\colon ob(D)\to Ord
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ord
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ord
              is the class of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinals"} ordinals
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              raises the degree.
            %li
              There exists an identity-reflecting functor (that is, it maps a morphism to an identity if and only if the morphism is itself an identity)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo :
                      %mi D
                      %mo →
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Ord
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d : D\to \mathbf{Ord}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Ord
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Ord}
              is the large poset of ordinals viewed as a category.
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reedy+category"} Reedy category
              (in particular an
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elegant+Reedy+category"} elegant Reedy category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_-
              consists only of identity maps (or equivalently
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_+
              is all of
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            In particular, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            is a direct category just when its strict order relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo &lt;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt
            is well-founded. Thus, direct categories can be seen as a categorification of well-founded relations.
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^{op}
            is a direct category, then we say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is an
            = succeed "." do
              %strong inverse category
          %h3#direct_versus_oneway_categories Direct versus one-way categories
          %p
            Of course, a direct category can have no nonidentity
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphisms"} endomorphisms
            = succeed "." do
              %strong one-way category
          %p
            A direct category is also necessarily
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeleton"} skeletal
          %p
            Conversely, a skeletal one-way category can have no cycles of nonidentity morphisms of any finite length, since by the one-way property all morphisms in such a cycle would be isomorphisms, hence endomorphisms by skeletality, and hence identities by the one-way property. Since any infinite chain in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+category"} finite category
            contains a cycle, we see that
          %ul
            %li A finite category is a direct category if and only if it is one-way and skeletal.
          %p Since this condition is self-dual, we also see that
          %ul
            %li A finite category is direct if and only if it is inverse.
          %p
            An infinite category can be one-way and skeletal without being direct or inverse, such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            with its usual ordering. However, if it additionally has
            = succeed "," do
              %strong finite fan-out
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            there are altogether only finitely many morphisms with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source"} source
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            (and arbitrary target), then it must be an inverse category, for any infinite non-cyclic chain would induce infinitely many distinct morphisms out of any of its objects by composition. In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FOLDS"} FOLDS
            %strong simple categories
            and used as signatures; thus
          %ul
            %li Any simple category (in the sense of FOLDS) is an inverse category.
          %p
            However, a category can be inverse without having finite fan-out. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            be an infinite set and consider the poset
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ∪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \cup \{\infty\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            having the discrete ordering (no nonidentity arrows) and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            being less than every element of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            is the source of infinitely many distinct arrows, but it is inverse, since there are clearly no chains of nonidentity arrows of length
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gt 1
          %h3#allowing_automorphisms Allowing automorphisms
          %p
            Some categories of geometric shapes, such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tree+category"} tree category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycle+category"} cycle category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+Reedy+category"} generalized Reedy category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            to be a
            %strong generalized direct category
            by replacing “identity” with “isomorphism” in the above definition. Thereby we obtain the following equivalent conditions for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            to be a generalized direct category.
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              contains no infinite descending chains of noninvertible morphisms
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo →
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo →
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo →
                      %mo ⋅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots \to\cdot \to\cdot\to\cdot
            %li
              The relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≺
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\prec b
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ob(D)
              defined by “there exists a noninvertible morphism from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              to
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-founded+relation"} well-founded
            %li
              There exists a function
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi ob
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi Ord
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\colon ob(D)\to Ord
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ord
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ord
              is the class of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinals"} ordinals
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              raises the degree.
            %li
              There exists an isomorphism-reflecting functor (that is, it maps a morphism to an isomorphism if and only if the morphism is itself an isomorphism)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo :
                      %mi D
                      %mo →
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Ord
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d : D\to \mathbf{Ord}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Ord
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Ord}
              is the large poset of ordinals viewed as a category.
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+Reedy+category"} generalized Reedy category
              in which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_-
              consists only of isomorphisms (or equivalently
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_+
              is all of
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            Of course,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
            are not generalized direct categories themselves, since they have degree-lowering degeneracies as well, but their full subcategories of coface maps are generalized-direct. More generally:
          %ul
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              is any generalized Reedy category, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_+
              is a generalized direct category.
          %h3#direct_categories_versus_finite_simplicial_sets Direct categories versus finite simplicial sets
          %p
            If a direct category has finitely many objects then its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            is a finite
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#model_structures Model structures
          %p
            Every direct category (and every inverse category) is in particular a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reedy+category"} Reedy category
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elegant+Reedy+category"} elegant Reedy category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reedy+model+structure"} Reedy model structure
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi M
                      %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^D
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#inside_the_simplex_category Inside the simplex category
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wide+subcategory"} wide subcategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_+
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
            on the injective maps is direct. That is, the category with objects being the totally ordered sets
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0\}, \{0, 1\}, \{0, 1, 2\}...
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            are
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-simplicial+objects"} semi-simplicial objects
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-simplicial+sets"} semi-simplicial sets
            as opposed to
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+objects"} simplicial objects
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
          %h3#the_direct_category_of_corollas The direct category of corollas
          %p
            We now define a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"}> universal
            ” generalized direct category which contains “all”
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+shapes+for+higher+structures"} geometric shapes for higher structures
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Borisov"} Borisov
          %p
            A functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon D\to E
            between generalized direct categories is called a
            %strong dependency
            if it is equivalent to the inclusion of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sieve"} sieve
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully faithful functor
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+fibration"} discrete fibration
            in the generalized sense of Street. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeleton"} skeletal
            (as they must be if they are
            %em non-generalized
            direct categories), then any dependency must be isomorphic to the inclusion of a sieve. One also usually works only with skeletal generalized direct categories, although the definition does not require it.
          %p
            If we regard direct categories as a categorification of well-founded relations, then dependencies are a categorification of injective
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simulation"} simulation
          %p
            Define a
            %strong corolla
            to be a generalized direct category with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+limit"} weakly
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            %strong vertex
            of the corolla. If a generalized direct category is finite and skeletal, then it is a corolla if and only if it has a unique object which is not the source of any noninvertible morphism. Corollas are the direct categories which it is most natural to regard as
            %em single
            geometric shapes; other direct categories are more like “pasting diagrams” of geometric shapes.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Corolla
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Corolla
            be the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
            skeletal corollas and dependencies.
          #UnivDirect.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Corolla
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Corolla
              is a (large) generalized direct category.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Suppose that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi d
                          %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 2
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi d
                          %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi d
                          %mn 0
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots \overset{d_2}{\to} C_2 \overset{d_1}{\to} C_1 \overset{d_0}{\to} C_0
              is a descending chain of noninvertible dependencies. Any dependency whose target is a corolla and whose image contains the vertex must be an equivalence, and hence an isomorphism if the corollas are skeletal; thus none of the dependencies
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{n+1} \to C_{n}
              can have the vertex in their image. Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_n\in C_0
              be the image of the vertex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star_n \in C_n
              under the composite
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi …
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_0\circ \dots \circ d_{n-1}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_{n+1} \to c_n
              for each
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_n(\star_{n+1}) \to \star_n
              which exists since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star_n
              is weakly terminal. This is a contradiction, since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0
              is a generalized direct category.
          %p
            Of course, the same is true for any subcategory of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Corolla
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Corolla
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FinCorolla
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinCorolla
            of
            %em finite
            corollas, which is moreover essentially small (though not finite).
          %p
            We next observe that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Corolla
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Corolla
            is the “universal” generalized direct category in a certain sense. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            be any generalized direct category; then the slice category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D/d
            is a corolla for any
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\in D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi d
                  %mo →
                  %mi d
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon d\to d'
            is a morphism in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            which is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi d
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi d
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_f\colon D/d \to D/d'
            is a dependency. Thus, if every morphism in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is monic, we have a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ext
                    %mi D
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi Corolla
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ext_D\colon D \to Corolla
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ext
                    %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ext_D(d)= D/d
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ext
                      %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ext_D(f)=\Sigma_f
          %p
            Now if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi d
                  %mo →
                  %mi d
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g\colon d\to d'
            are parallel morphisms and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_f=\Sigma_g
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = \Sigma_f(id_d) = \Sigma_g(id_d) = g
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ext
                    %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ext_D
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is a direct category in which all morphisms are monic, and in which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi d
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi d
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D/d \cong D/d'
            implies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d=d'
            (which includes many examples), then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is equivalent to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Corolla
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Corolla
          %p
            This subcategory of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Corolla
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Corolla
            is usually
            %em not
            full, however. In particular, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\in D
            the corolla
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D/d
            will generally admit more automorphisms than
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            has in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is a non-generalized direct category, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            has no nontrivial automorphisms, whereas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D/d
            generally will. In particular, if the objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            have any sort of “orientation” or “labeling,” then this information is forgotten by the functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ext
                      %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ext_D
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/test+category"} test category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+category"}> filtered category
                ,
